uhCkkZMnV5ZfezqdTCwsLbWwTF1qcqh6EALJmpzS.description:
{
  "outcome": "Smallness is a clarity threshold, not a guess and not something a person sets by hand. A leaf's readiness to be a Small is computed from multiple signals and cached on the node, because the more complex a bottom-level item is, the less likely anyone is clear about it. Most signals are genuine gradients — how complex, how wide the agent's change, how large the review surface, how much design latitude is still open. A few are binary blockers — an unresolved unknown, no evaluable criterion — that veto readiness outright. Reaching small-enough leaves is itself part of achieving clarity.",
  "handle": "small-readiness"
}

uhCkkj_5P2u8gL63ny6W_MYg0EKq8Ty5L_TuB9cF.description:
{
  "outcome": "A leaf's smallness readiness is a single cached value on the node — an instance of the node's computed results (the computed-results outcome b6 in branch B), not an authored field. It aggregates the readiness signals into a gradient and is rendered as the leaf's color, so one glance across the map shows which leaves are clear enough to build and which still need breakdown. Any active binary blocker clamps the gradient to not-ready regardless of the other signals.",
  "spec": "computed.smallReadiness = aggregate(gradient signals) in 0..1, clamped to 0 by any active binary blocker. Recomputed when its input fields/verdicts change and mapped to a color ramp on the leaf.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "Changing a signal recomputes the cached readiness and updates the leaf color; an active binary blocker forces the not-ready color",
      "evaluator": "executable"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkQd5oyrQEBPMeIpbV5Cpq3DO6fMbYNWx_DuG.description:
{
  "outcome": "Every leaf shows a complexity/uncertainty estimate — the agile-style signal — as a real gradient. A higher estimate lowers readiness and, past the breakdown threshold, pushes the leaf toward decomposition; a low estimate, roughly one small unit, raises readiness.",
  "signalType": "gradient",
  "spec": "Extend smallsEstimate into a continuous contribution to computed.smallReadiness; surface the estimate and the breakdown threshold on the leaf.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "A higher complexity estimate measurably lowers the cached readiness gradient",
      "evaluator": "executable"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkk599nwKNK-DIdwNq5_-9zTr_KTtTIdXCpSYm.description:
{
  "outcome": "The estimated spread of the change a building agent would make is a gradient input to readiness: a tightly-scoped single change set reads as ready; a change spanning many unrelated areas lowers readiness and suggests a split.",
  "signalType": "gradient",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "An agent's scope-spread assessment contributes proportionally to the readiness gradient",
      "evaluator": "executable"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkSZs8a34CC2HWIB6DXxv8JkXoNPdWcFaUPWm.description:
{
  "outcome": "The size of the review surface — how much a reviewer must hold in mind to verify the finished leaf against its criteria — is a gradient input to readiness. A leaf reviewable in one sitting reads as ready; a larger surface lowers readiness.",
  "signalType": "gradient",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "A reviewer's single-sitting assessment maps onto the readiness gradient",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkk2sP0YbW8tnu-HoySANBJP0nuB-rnw__j--V.description:
{
  "outcome": "How much design latitude remains open in the leaf's spec is a gradient input to readiness: a spec at implementation latitude with no open choices reads as ready; each unresolved design decision lowers readiness, because open design hides decomposition.",
  "signalType": "gradient",
  "spec": "Map the spec's latitude marker and its count of open decisions (branch A3) to a readiness contribution.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "Reviewer-confirmed open design decisions lower the readiness gradient",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkAOEDBDDuTh8RAIek0Ka38ubdc3L9snRqSFN.description:
{
  "outcome": "A leaf with no completion criterion concrete enough to evaluate is, by definition, not yet clear — so the absence of any evaluable criterion is a binary blocker that clamps readiness to not-ready, whatever the gradient signals say.",
  "signalType": "binary blocker",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "A leaf with zero evaluable criteria shows the not-ready color until a concrete criterion is added",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkr3a1voNSPuUaVHga-ucXZRVoBMpB1ECP7ga.description:
{
  "outcome": "An unresolved open unknown is exactly where clarity is missing, so any unresolved known-unknown on a leaf is a binary blocker: readiness is clamped to not-ready until the unknown is resolved or moved into a child outcome. (Room remains to flag suspected unknown-unknowns.)",
  "signalType": "binary blocker",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "A leaf with a recorded open unknown stays not-ready until it is resolved or pushed into a child outcome",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkR2BG4yBaauG7F_UG2-zVmD2f-zF5iAObgJb.description:
{
  "outcome": "When a leaf's readiness is low or a binary blocker is active, the AI assistant proposes a concrete decomposition — child outcomes that each look more likely to be ready — for the human to approve. Reaching small-enough leaves becomes a guided, conversational step of achieving clarity.",
  "spec": "On low readiness or an active blocker, the assistant (branch F) drafts child outcomes and surfaces them via the proposal/approval path (branches D3/F2).",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "A low-readiness or blocked leaf yields an assistant-proposed breakdown the human can accept, modify, or reject",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ],
  "principle": "Humans author intent; proposed decompositions are inert until a human accepts them."
}

uhCkkxPEUXEuyqQIhljTpjrQarDrDuZ98BCQMUbo.description:
{
  "outcome": "Every change a person can make to a clarity tree — editing any field, restructuring nodes, attaching artifacts, marking completion — works under real-time multi-user co-editing without clobbering, lost updates, or stale views. Held high-level and low-priority for now: a standing concern, not yet decomposed into smalls. Today's per-description editing presence is the seed to generalize across every field and edit."
}

uhCkk7RGevjJArcRb0EHyGbnHOZHEEKaIUQ7L1-U.description:
{
  "outcome": "After a diff is applied, each touched node renders a transient glowy outline — visually distinct from the normal selection border — so a human sees which nodes the agent changed, with no selection side-effects.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "Applying a diff renders an amber glow on every touched node, visibly distinct from the blue selection outline, and clicking the background clears it",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkky6xJNeAj2LzyP0S3W72PSuGJs7Go_L6DSxv.description:
{
  "outcome": "The applied diff's change counts render as +/~/− badges on each changed node, so the human sees the scale and location of every change at a glance.",
  "spec": "Each changed node renders chips overlapping its top-right corner while diff-review mode (the glow) is active: green +N (items added to the node — tasks, clarity fields, new children), amber ~N (items edited — statement, status, fields, task toggles; matches the glow color), red −N (items removed, including deleted children: a deleted node badges its surviving parent). A brand-new node gets a single green 'new' chip. The popup chips remain as the whole-diff outcome-level summary.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "After Apply, every changed node carries +/~/− chips at its top-right whose counts match what the diff did to that node",
      "evaluator": "human"
    },
    {
      "statement": "A deleted node's surviving parent shows a red −N chip",
      "evaluator": "human"
    },
    {
      "statement": "perOutcomeChangeStats unit tests cover task, field, statement, status, child-add and child-delete counting",
      "evaluator": "executable"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkGeTZ_C2A0e5JRFiyXvU9KtyBwjqabAKt0ar.description:
{
  "outcome": "After a diff is applied, the viewport zooms/pans to fit ALL changed nodes (their bounding box), with no particular node selected — replacing the pan-to-one that jumped to blank space.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "After Apply, every changed node is on screen at once with comfortable margin, nothing selected, glow visible on each",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkXF4sFOZclP5yq2xxwImIGRoGALPDbUrMLC_.description:
{
  "outcome": "A node counts as changed whether its fields changed or its leaf checklist (tasks) changed — the diff compares the whole outcome, so both register."
}

uhCkk883cAjI4Oir7zRSse3y9WgkTRRReR7RcXLQ.description:
{
  "outcome": "Between last export to llm a human may have made changes.  Importing an LLM's changes should not override them.  If there are merge conflicts this should be flagged.   i.e. it should not just be importing a whole tree, it should be integrating the specific changes of the diff.",
  "spec": "Apply performs a three-way merge: the agent's diff is compared against both the live tree and the last-export baseline (the snapshot saved when the human exported). For each node the agent updates — if the human also changed it since the export (live differs from baseline) and the agent's value differs from the live value, that node is a conflict: flagged for review, never silently overwritten. Non-conflicting agent changes apply normally.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "A node edited by the human after export and also updated by the agent's diff is flagged as a conflict, and the human's live value is not silently overwritten",
      "evaluator": "human"
    },
    {
      "statement": "Merge logic is pure and unit-tested: agent-only change applies; human-only change persists; both-changed flags a conflict",
      "evaluator": "executable"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkk9gYB85eOP5fH9UGJztvVcdidWPLjDDSwPgb.description:
{
  "outcome": "There should be a close button on the notification popup that's displayed after import or export.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "The status popup shown after export or apply has a ✕ button that dismisses it (and the badge with it)",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkksp-1vurbCb7yenZAY8cJ7qZwfE3hx8W99eh.description:
{
  "outcome": "An LLM works with the live clarity tree through a direct, in-app API — reading current state and proposing typed edits — instead of the file-based diff round-trip (branch I export/import). Because it reads and proposes against the live tree, the tolerant-merge import (branch I, \"tolerant to changes since last export\") becomes redundant: there is no stale export to reconcile.",
  "handle": "llm-api",
  "spec": "A flow view of the live loop belongs here (a diagram, not a list): the LLM reads the tree for context, proposes typed edits, the human reviews and commits them, and the LLM executes the work a ready leaf specifies. The detailed specs live on the leaves below.",
  "principle": "The human is the only committer. The LLM reads and proposes; it never writes to the DHT directly.",
  "artifacts": [
    {
      "type": "reference",
      "label": "flow diagram: the live LLM↔tree loop (placeholder)",
      "uri": ""
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkaL_nVKdHM3cuevXR25gQAtpXtbh0Njsl0t3.description:
{
  "outcome": "The LLM reads the live tree through a read tool (read_tree): nodes, typed fields, connections, and authored achievement status. This is the read half of the MCP-style tool surface; the propose/write half lives in the conversational-editing branch's draft pipeline, so reads are a general capability while writes always go through review. The read shape reuses the agent-consumable export (branch D). Computed achievement/readiness is surfaced here once branch B's computed layer exists — read_tree exposes whatever the snapshot holds; today that is authored scope/achievementStatus.",
  "spec": "read_tree() -> the live project snapshot (outcomes with their typed fields, connections, tags, authored scope/achievementStatus), typed so the harness targets it without scraping the UI. (read_tree and propose_edits together form the MCP-style surface; propose_edits is specified under the draft pipeline.) Computed achievement/readiness appears here once branch B produces it — a relies-on, not built in this leaf.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "The harness calls read_tree and receives the live project's nodes, typed fields, connections and authored status",
      "evaluator": "executable",
      "evidence": "2026-06-17: harness called mcp__acorn__read_tree and received the live ProjectSnapshot — 70 outcomes, 69 connections, 4 tags; top-level keys {projectMeta, outcomes, connections, outcomeMembers, outcomeComments, entryPoints, tags}. Each outcome carries its typed fields in the description (parseable OutcomeFields) plus scope.achievementStatus. Result was 137,556 chars — too large for the harness context window in one piece, but fully received and parsed."
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkHkOAZcdB2kaFeqblICiZs7Hs1v3DfaiKKb9.description:
{
  "outcome": "Distinct from improving the clarity OF the tree, this is doing the work the tree clarifies: when a human manually triggers a ready leaf, the LLM reads that leaf's outcome, spec and completion criteria, performs the work (e.g. writes the code), then posts results — completion verdicts and produced artifacts — back onto the node (relies on branches D and E). Manual trigger is the prototype step before readiness assessment (branch G) gates it automatically. A clear, unambiguous leaf is exactly what makes this easy to direct.",
  "handle": "do-it-trigger",
  "spec": "A 'Do this leaf' action on a sufficiently-clear leaf hands its clarity fields to the harness as the work spec; the harness executes and returns artifacts + verdicts, which attach to the node (relies on branches D2/D3, E). No automatic triggering yet — the human picks the leaf.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "A human triggers a ready leaf; the LLM performs the work and posts at least one artifact and a completion verdict back to that node",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkNIhWkjN6-3PxlYV4ZkcTYdSSLBefF2oAVb2.description:
{
  "outcome": "A human directs tree edits by conversing with the LLM and reviewing what it proposes before anything commits — the same conversation that built this tree, now happening inside Acorn against the live tree, with a human-approved commit gate.",
  "spec": "A flow view of the conversation-to-commit path belongs here: a request reaches the harness, its proposed edits open a draft, the human reviews and edits, and confirmation commits. The detailed specs live on the leaves below.",
  "principle": "Proposed edits are inert until the human confirms them (plan-mode contract).",
  "artifacts": [
    {
      "type": "reference",
      "label": "flow diagram: conversation → draft → commit (placeholder)",
      "uri": ""
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkyWnYfKEcJoBpJJblucgld8ouBNBwrvlxSTV.description:
{
  "outcome": "Acorn embeds an ACP (Agent Client Protocol) client that connects to a local LLM harness (e.g. a local Claude Code / agent process). An in-app chat panel sends the human's message plus the live tree context to the harness and shows its reply. When the reply includes proposed edits, the permission seams are plumbed to hand them to the draft pipeline; this node is responsible for the conversation and transport.",
  "spec": "Embed an ACP client; the chat panel sends {user message + live tree context} to the harness and renders the reply (message, thought, plan, and tool_call streams). Proposed edits in the reply are passed to the draft pipeline via plumbed permission seams. Transport is abstracted: DevSidecarHarnessClient (WebSocket over dev-server) for development, MossHarnessClient (Weave host API) for production.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "A human sends a message in the in-app chat and sees the harness's reply; any proposed edits in it are handed to the draft pipeline",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ],
  "artifacts": [
    {
      "type": "workproduct",
      "label": "HarnessChat.tsx — in-app chat panel with streaming updates, tree context injection, selection context",
